use std
into flow_plate.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with fp_ is exported). It is a port of the 2.5D plate flow solver in cad/flow_plate.js: the conductance matrix assembly (`assemble` with `csr`), the smoothed aggregation algebraic multigrid setup (`amg` with `diagOf`, `csr`, `transpose` and `spmm`), the multigrid cycle (`vcyc`, `gs`, `mv`) and the preconditioned conjugate gradient solver (`pcg`). Results are compared bit for bit with the JavaScript, so every floating-point formula keeps the JavaScript evaluation order exactly: each sum starts at its stated first value and adds or subtracts its terms one at a time in loop order, products and quotients group left to right as written, and nothing is fused, reordered or vectorized. Reals are f64. A matrix is compressed sparse rows: row i holds entries k from rp[i] up to but not including rp[i+1], with column ci[k] and value va[k].

```amni
extern fn fp_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "fp_view_f64";
extern fn fp_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "fp_view_i32";
extern fn fp_view_u8(id i32, xs u8[:]) = "fp_view_u8";
extern fn fp_view_f32(id i32, xs f32[:]) = "fp_view_f32";
extern fn fp_sin(x real) real = "fp_sin";
extern fn fp_cos(x real) real = "fp_cos";
extern fn fp_atan2(y real, x real) real = "fp_atan2";
```

Struct `Lv` is one multigrid level, holding offsets into the pools: n (rows), nnz (entries), rp (offset in `IP` of n+1 row starts), ci (offset in `IP` of nnz columns), va (offset in `FP` of nnz values), d (offset in `FP` of n diagonal entries), kind (0 a multigrid level with a prolongation, 1 the dense Cholesky level, 2 the Gauss-Seidel level), stall (bool), nc (coarse rows), pnz (prolongation entries), prp (offset in `IP` of n+1 prolongation row starts), pci (offset in `IP` of pnz prolongation columns), pva (offset in `FP` of pnz prolongation values), wr (offset in `FP` of n residual entries), b and x (offsets in `FP` of n entries each for the right side and solution handed down to this level, or −1 on level 0), ch (offset in `FP` of n·n Cholesky entries, or −1) and y (offset in `FP` of n entries, or −1). Offsets that a level does not use are −1.

Module state: `NN`, `NA`, `NB`, `NPT` and `NU` (int: pixels, pixel columns, pixel rows, ports and unknowns). `ACT` (u8), `UID` (i32), `POF` (i32) and `CE` (f64) are growable arrays of NN pixel inputs. `CRP` (i32), `CCI` (i32) and `CVA` (f64) are the current matrix. `BP` (f64, NPT·NU, entry k·NU+u), `PV` (f64 port pressures), `PB` (f64 right side) and `XS` (f64 solution) are growable arrays. `RES` (real) is the last residual. `IP` (i32) and `FP` (f64) are the pools, `QI` (i32) and `QF` (f64) are scratch arrays for moving a pool, and `LV` is the growable array of levels, finest first. Scratch growable arrays: `AG`, `A1`, `MK`, `RK`, `TRP`, `TCJ`, `PRP`, `PCJ`, `SP`, `SJ`, `APP` and `APJ` (i32), and `DF`, `PX`, `PY`, `RV`, `TCV`, `PCV`, `SV`, `SA` and `APV` (f64). `PR`, `PZ`, `PP` and `PQ` are the conjugate gradient vectors (f64). Small helpers return pool slices: `ipv(off, len)` is `IP` from off for len entries and `fpv(off, len)` is `FP` from off for len entries.

`ip_add(len) int` returns the old length o of `IP` and grows `IP` to o+len entries with one `resize` filled with zeros; when `IP.cap` is below o+len it first moves the pool: `QI` is emptied and gets a copy of `IP` with one `append`, `IP` is emptied and `IP.reserve(2*(o+len))` runs, then `IP` gets `QI` back with one `append` and `QI` is emptied. `fp_add(len) int` does the same for `FP` with `QF`.

The exported function `fp_grid(n i32, na i32, nb i32, np i32)` stores `NN`, `NA`, `NB` and `NPT`; `ACT`, `UID`, `POF` and `CE` are each emptied and then resized to n zeros, `PV` is emptied and then resized to the larger of 1 and np zeros; it reports `fp_view_u8(1, ACT)`, `fp_view_i32(2, UID)`, `fp_view_i32(3, POF)`, `fp_view_f64(4, CE)` and `fp_view_f64(5, PV)`.

The exported function `fp_asm(nu i32)` is exactly flow_plate.js `assemble` with `csr`. `NU` becomes nu; `CRP` is emptied and then resized to nu+1 zeros; `CCI` and `CVA` are each emptied and then resized to 5·nu zeros and are written by index with a running count m starting at 0; `BP` is emptied and then resized to NPT·nu zeros. The pixels are visited in increasing order by two nested loops, j from 0 to NB minus 1 outside and i from 0 to NA minus 1 inside, with p equal to j·NA plus i; u is `UID`[p] and the pixel is skipped when u is below 0. dg starts at 0.0. The neighbours q of p are taken in this order: p−1 when i is above 0, p+1 when i is below NA−1, p−NA when j is above 0 and p+NA when j is below NB−1. A neighbour is skipped when `ACT`[q] is 0. Otherwise x is `CE`[p], y is `CE`[q] and T is ((2.0·x)·y) divided by (x plus y) when x is above 0 and y is above 0, and 0.0 otherwise; the neighbour is skipped unless T is above 0. dg grows by T; when `UID`[q] is at least 0, `CCI`[m] becomes `UID`[q], `CVA`[m] becomes −T and m grows by one; otherwise `BP`[`POF`[q]·nu plus u] grows by T. After the neighbours, `CCI`[m] becomes u, `CVA`[m] becomes dg, m grows by one and `CRP`[u+1] becomes m. Finally `CCI` and `CVA` are resized to m entries.

The exported function `fp_load(n i32, nnz i32)` sets `NU` to n; `CRP` is emptied and then resized to n+1 zeros, `CCI` and `CVA` to nnz zeros, `PB` and `XS` to n zeros; it reports `fp_view_i32(8, CRP)`, `fp_view_i32(9, CCI)`, `fp_view_f64(10, CVA)`, `fp_view_f64(7, PB)` and `fp_view_f64(6, XS)`.

`spmm(arp, aci, ava, brp, bci, bva, n int, m int, op, oj, ov) int` multiplies the sparse matrix A (n rows: row starts arp, columns aci, values ava) by B (row starts brp, columns bci, values bva, m columns) into op (n+1 row starts), oj and ov (written by index), exactly as flow_plate.js `spmm`, and returns the entry count nz. All array parameters are slices; op, oj and ov are writable i32, i32 and f64 slices already sized by the caller. `MK` is emptied and then resized to m entries of −1 and `SA` is emptied and then resized to m zeros. nz starts at 0 and op[0] is 0. For each row i in order: s is nz; for each entry k of row i of A in order, a is ava[k] and r is aci[k]; for each entry q of row r of B in order, c is bci[q]; when `MK`[c] is below s, `MK`[c] becomes nz, oj[nz] becomes c, `SA`[c] becomes 0.0 and nz grows by one; then `SA`[c] grows by a·bva[q]. After the row, for each q from s to nz minus 1, ov[q] becomes `SA`[oj[q]], and op[i+1] becomes nz.

`spcnt(arp, aci, brp, bci, n int, m int) int` (all i32 slices) is the exact number of entries `spmm` produces for the same A rows and B pattern: `MK` is emptied and then resized to m entries of −1 and nz starts at 0; for each row i below n, each entry k of row i of A in order with r equal to aci[k], and each entry q of row r of B in order with c equal to bci[q]: when `MK`[c] is not i, `MK`[c] becomes i and nz grows by one. It returns nz.

`chol(l)` builds the dense Cholesky factor of level l exactly as the dense branch of flow_plate.js `amg`. With n the level's n: the level's ch becomes `fp_add(n·n)` and its y becomes `fp_add(n)`, both before any pool slice is taken; kind becomes 1. With M the ch block, rp, ci and va the level's matrix slices: for each row i and each entry k of it in order, M[i·n+ci[k]] is set to va[k]. Then for each i, M[i·n+i] becomes M[i·n+i] plus ((1e-14 times the absolute value of M[i·n+i]) plus 1e-300). Then for each column j in order: s is M[j·n+j]; for k below j, s gets M[j·n+k] times M[j·n+k] subtracted; M[j·n+j] becomes the square root of the larger of s and 1e-300 (NaN when s is NaN); then for each i from j+1 below n, t is M[i·n+j], for k below j t gets M[i·n+k] times M[j·n+k] subtracted, and M[i·n+j] becomes t divided by M[j·n+j].

`new_level(n, nnz) int` appends a level to `LV` and returns its index: its n and nnz are n and nnz, rp is `ip_add(n+1)`, ci is `ip_add(nnz)`, va is `fp_add(nnz)`, d is `fp_add(n)`, b and x are each `fp_add(n)`, kind is 0, stall is false and every other offset is −1, with nc and pnz 0.

The exported function `fp_amg(th0 real)` builds the levels from the current matrix exactly as flow_plate.js `amg(A, th0)`. `IP`, `FP` and `LV` are emptied; with n equal to `NU` and nnz equal to `CRP`[n], `IP.reserve(3·nnz+8·n+4096)` and `FP.reserve(3·nnz+8·n+4096)` run. Level 0 is `new_level(n, nnz)`, except that its b and x are −1 (no pool space), and its rp, ci and va get copies of `CRP`, `CCI` and `CVA`. h starts at 1.0. Then it loops forever over the last level l of `LV`, re-reading n, nnz and the level's slices rp, ci, va and d at the top of every round (a pool slice is never held across `ip_add`, `fp_add`, `chol` or `new_level`):

1. Diagonal, exactly as `diagOf`: every d[i] is set to 0.0, then for each row i and each entry k of it in order whose column is i, d[i] becomes va[k]. th is th0 times h.
2. When n is at most 400, or the number of finished levels (`LV.len` minus 1) is above 24, or the level's stall is true and n is at most 2500: `chol(l)` and the loop ends.
3. Entry k of row i is strong when ci[k] is not i and the absolute value of va[k] is at least th times the square root of the absolute value of (d[i] times d[ci[k]]). `AG` is emptied and then resized to n entries of −1 and nc starts at 0. First pass, for each row i in order with `AG`[i] below 0: ok starts true; for each entry k of row i in order, when k is strong and `AG`[ci[k]] is at least 0, ok becomes false and the scan stops; when ok is still true, `AG`[i] becomes nc, every strong entry k of row i sets `AG`[ci[k]] to nc, and nc grows by one. Then `A1` is emptied and gets a copy of `AG` with one `append`. Second pass, for each row i in order with `A1`[i] below 0: b starts at −1 and bv at 0.0; for each entry k of row i in order that is strong, with `A1`[ci[k]] at least 0 and the absolute value of va[k] above bv, bv becomes that absolute value and b becomes `A1`[ci[k]]; `AG`[i] becomes b when b is at least 0, and otherwise nc, with nc then growing by one.
4. When nc (as a real) is at least n times 0.9, or nc is below 1: when n is at most 2500 the level's stall becomes true and the next round starts with the same level; otherwise the level's kind becomes 2 and the loop ends.
5. `DF` is emptied and then resized to n zeros; for each row i, `DF`[i] starts at d[i] and, for each entry k of row i in order whose column is not i and that is not strong, gets va[k] subtracted.
6. `PX` is emptied and then resized to n zeros with `PX`[i] equal to fp_sin((i as a real)·1.7 plus 0.3); `PY` is emptied and then resized to n zeros; rho starts at 1.0. Twelve times: for each row i, s starts at `DF`[i] times `PX`[i] and gets va[k] times `PX`[ci[k]] added for each strong entry k of row i in order, and `PY`[i] becomes s divided by `DF`[i]; then nx and ny start at 0.0 and, for each i in order, nx grows by `PX`[i] times `PX`[i] and ny grows by `PY`[i] times `PY`[i]; rho becomes the square root of (ny divided by nx); s is 1.0 divided by the square root of ny; and every `PX`[i] becomes `PY`[i] times s.
7. om is 4.0 divided by (3.0 times rho). The prolongation P (n rows, nc columns) goes into `PRP` (emptied and then resized to n+1 zeros), `PCJ` and `PCV` (each emptied and then gets `reserve`(nnz+n), filled by `push` with a count pz starting at 0). For each row i in order, the row is a small ordered list of keys and values: `RK` and `RV` are each emptied and then resized to (number of entries of row i) plus 1 zeros, with a length rl. The first key is `AG`[i] with value 1.0 minus om, so rl is 1. For each strong entry k of row i in order: c is `AG`[ci[k]]; when c is already one of the first rl keys at position e, prev is `RV`[e] when that value is neither 0 nor NaN and 0.0 otherwise, and `RV`[e] becomes prev minus ((om times va[k]) divided by `DF`[i]); when c is not a key, `RK`[rl] becomes c, `RV`[rl] becomes 0.0 minus ((om times va[k]) divided by `DF`[i]) and rl grows by one. Then the rl keys and values are pushed in list order onto `PCJ` and `PCV`, pz growing by one each, and `PRP`[i+1] becomes pz.
8. The transpose R of P (nc rows) goes into `TRP` (emptied and then resized to nc+1 zeros), `TCJ` and `TCV` (each emptied and then resized to pz zeros), exactly as `transpose`: for each prolongation entry k below pz, `TRP`[`PCJ`[k]+1] grows by one; then for each c below nc, `TRP`[c+1] grows by `TRP`[c]; `MK` is emptied and gets the first nc entries of `TRP` with one `append` and serves as the fill position; for each row i of P in order and each entry k of it in order, with c equal to `PCJ`[k], `TCJ`[`MK`[c]] becomes i, `TCV`[`MK`[c]] becomes `PCV`[k] and `MK`[c] grows by one.
9. AP is `spmm` of the level matrix (rp, ci, va, n rows) by P (`PRP`, `PCJ`, `PCV`, nc columns) into `APP` (emptied and then resized to n+1 zeros), `APJ` and `APV` (each emptied and then resized to `spcnt`(rp, ci, `PRP`, `PCJ`, n, nc) zeros); its count is anz. Then the coarse matrix is `spmm` of R (`TRP`, `TCJ`, `TCV`, nc rows) by AP (`APP`, `APJ`, `APV`, nc columns) into `SP` (emptied and then resized to nc+1 zeros), `SJ` and `SV` (each emptied and then resized to `spcnt`(`TRP`, `TCJ`, `APP`, `APJ`, nc, nc) zeros); its count is cz.
10. The level stores P: its nc becomes nc, pnz becomes pz, prp becomes `ip_add(n+1)` with a copy of `PRP`, pci becomes `ip_add(pz)` with the first pz entries of `PCJ`, pva becomes `fp_add(pz)` with the first pz entries of `PCV`, and wr becomes `fp_add(n)`. Then the new level `new_level(nc, cz)` gets the first nc+1 entries of `SP` in its rp, the first cz entries of `SJ` in its ci and the first cz entries of `SV` in its va. h becomes h times 0.5 and the next round starts with the new level.

`gs(l, b, x, back)` (b and x are f64 slices, back is a bool) is one Gauss-Seidel sweep on level l, exactly as flow_plate.js `gs`: for q from 0 below n, i is n−1−q when back is true and otherwise q; s starts at b[i] and, for each entry k of row i in order whose column is not i, gets va[k] times x[ci[k]] subtracted; x[i] becomes s divided by d[i].

`cyc(l, b, x)` (b and x are writable f64 slices of n entries) is exactly flow_plate.js `vcyc` for level l. When kind is 1, with M the ch block and y the level's y slice: forward, for each i in order, s starts at b[i], for k below i gets M[i·n+k] times y[k] subtracted, and y[i] becomes s divided by M[i·n+i]; backward, for i from n−1 down to 0, s starts at y[i], for k from i+1 below n gets M[k·n+i] times x[k] subtracted, and x[i] becomes s divided by M[i·n+i]; then it returns. When kind is 2: every x[i] becomes 0.0, then `gs(l, b, x, false)`, `gs(l, b, x, true)`, `gs(l, b, x, false)`, `gs(l, b, x, true)`, and it returns. Otherwise, with nc the level's nc, r the level's wr slice and bc and xc the slices of the next level's b and x (nc entries each): every x[i] becomes 0.0; `gs(l, b, x, false)`; for each row i, s starts at 0.0 and adds va[k] times x[ci[k]] for each entry k in order, and r[i] becomes b[i] minus s; every bc[c] becomes 0.0, then for each row i in order and each prolongation entry k of row i in order, bc[pci[k]] grows by pva[k] times r[i]; `cyc(l+1, bc, xc)`; then for each row i, s starts at 0.0 and adds pva[k] times xc[pci[k]] for each prolongation entry k of row i in order, and x[i] becomes x[i] plus s; finally `gs(l, b, x, true)`.

`mvc(v, o)` (f64 slices) multiplies the current matrix (`CRP`, `CCI`, `CVA`) by v: for each row i, s starts at 0.0 and adds `CVA`[k] times v[`CCI`[k]] for each entry k in order, and o[i] becomes s.

`pcg(tol real, maxit int) int` solves the current matrix times `XS` equals `PB`, starting from the values in `XS`, exactly as flow_plate.js `pcg` with the levels in `LV`, and returns the iteration count it+1 (or 0). n is `NU`. `PR`, `PZ`, `PP` and `PQ` are each emptied and then resized to n zeros. `mvc(XS, PR)`; bn starts at 0.0; for each i in order, `PR`[i] becomes `PB`[i] minus `PR`[i] and bn grows by `PB`[i] times `PB`[i]; bn becomes its square root. When bn is 0 or NaN, every `XS`[i] becomes 0.0, `RES` becomes 0.0 and it returns 0. Otherwise `cyc(0, PR, PZ)`; `PP` becomes a copy of `PZ` (element by element); rz starts at 0.0 and grows by `PR`[i] times `PZ`[i] for each i; it starts at 0 and rn at 1.0. While it is below maxit: `mvc(PP, PQ)`; pq starts at 0.0 and grows by `PP`[i] times `PQ`[i] for each i; al is rz divided by pq; rn becomes 0.0 and for each i in order `XS`[i] becomes `XS`[i] plus al times `PP`[i], `PR`[i] becomes `PR`[i] minus al times `PQ`[i] and rn grows by `PR`[i] times `PR`[i]; rn becomes the square root of rn divided by bn; when rn is below tol the loop stops (it is not incremented); otherwise `cyc(0, PR, PZ)`, rz2 starts at 0.0 and grows by `PR`[i] times `PZ`[i] for each i, be is rz2 divided by rz, rz becomes rz2, every `PP`[i] becomes `PZ`[i] plus be times `PP`[i], and it grows by one. `RES` becomes rn and it returns it plus 1.

The exported function `fp_pcg(tol real, maxit i32) i32` returns `pcg(tol, maxit)`. The exported function `fp_res() real` returns `RES`.

The exported function `fp_solve(tol real, maxit i32) i32` builds the right side exactly as the `solve` closure of flow_plate.js `solvePlate`: `PB` is emptied and then resized to `NU` zeros; for each port k from 0 to `NPT` minus 1 in order, when `PV`[k] is neither 0 nor NaN, every `PB`[u] grows by `PV`[k] times `BP`[k·NU plus u] (u in order). `XS` is emptied and then resized to `NU` zeros. Then `fp_view_f64(6, XS)` and `fp_view_f64(7, PB)` are reported and it returns `pcg(tol, maxit)`.

Rasterizing. More module state: `TL` (f64, the local vertex coordinates, 3 per vertex: across, along and height), `TI` (i32, 3 vertex numbers per triangle), `BC`, `BN`, `BF`, `NF` and `SK` (i32), `SE`, `SX` and `EXT` (u8), `PHI`, `DEP` and `PH0` (f32) growable arrays, and the reals `GA0`, `GB0`, `GZC`, `GDX` and ints `GNA`, `GNB`. jmax(a real, b real) real is NaN when a or b is NaN, otherwise b when b is above a, otherwise a.

The exported function `fp_rast_in(nv i32, nt i32)` empties `TL` and then resizes it to 3·nv zeros, empties `TI` and then resizes it to 3·nt zeros, and reports `fp_view_f64(11, TL)` and `fp_view_i32(12, TI)`.

Struct `Rg` holds ok (bool), i0, i1, j0 and j1 (int). `rng(t int, eps real) Rg` is exactly the `rng` closure of flow_plate.js `rasterPlate`: a, b and c are 3·`TI`[3·t], 3·`TI`[3·t+1] and 3·`TI`[3·t+2]; ax and ay are `TL`[a] and `TL`[a+1], bx and by are `TL`[b] and `TL`[b+1], cx and cy are `TL`[c] and `TL`[c+1]. ok is false when the absolute value of ((by−cy)·(ax−cx) plus (cx−bx)·(ay−cy)) is below eps. Otherwise i0 is the larger of 0 and floor((min(ax, bx, cx) minus `GA0`) divided by `GDX`), i1 is the smaller of `GNA`−1 and floor((max(ax, bx, cx) minus `GA0`) divided by `GDX`), j0 is the larger of 0 and floor((min(ay, by, cy) minus `GB0`) divided by `GDX`) and j1 is the smaller of `GNB`−1 and floor((max(ay, by, cy) minus `GB0`) divided by `GDX`), each floor converted to int.

`col(p int, px real, py real) real` is exactly the `col` closure of rasterPlate: top starts at −1e30 and hit at false; for each k from `BC`[p] below `BC`[p+1] in order: t is `BN`[k]; a, b and c are 3·`TI`[3·t], 3·`TI`[3·t+1] and 3·`TI`[3·t+2]; ax, ay, bx, by, cx and cy are read from `TL` as in rng; d is (by−cy)·(ax−cx) plus (cx−bx)·(ay−cy); l1 is ((by−cy)·(px−cx) plus (cx−bx)·(py−cy)) divided by d; l2 is ((cy−ay)·(px−cx) plus (ax−cx)·(py−cy)) divided by d; l3 is (1.0 minus l1) minus l2; the triangle is skipped when l1, l2 or l3 is below −1e-7; otherwise hit becomes true and top becomes jmax(top, (l1·`TL`[a+2] plus l2·`TL`[b+2]) plus l3·`TL`[c+2]). It returns −1.0 when hit is false, and otherwise jmax(0.0, `GZC` minus top).

The exported function `fp_rast(na i32, nb i32, a0 real, b0 real, zb real, zc real, dx real, eps real, ss i32)` is exactly flow_plate.js `rasterPlate` after its vertex loop. It stores `GNA`, `GNB`, `GA0`, `GB0`, `GZC` and `GDX`; n is na·nb and nt is the length of `TI` divided by 3.

1. Binning: `BC` is emptied and then resized to n+1 zeros; for each triangle t in order whose rng(t, eps) is ok, for j from j0 to j1 and i from i0 to i1 (inclusive, j outside), `BC`[j·na+i+1] grows by one. Then for each p below n, `BC`[p+1] grows by `BC`[p]. `BN` is emptied and then resized to `BC`[n] zeros; `BF` is emptied and gets the first n entries of `BC` with one `append`; for each triangle t in order whose rng is ok, for the same j and i, `BN`[`BF`[j·na+i]] becomes t and `BF`[j·na+i] grows by one.
2. hh is zc minus zb, tz is 1e-4·hh plus 1e-6, s2 is ss·ss, sa is na·ss and sb is nb·ss. `PHI` and `DEP` (f32) are each emptied and then resized to n zeros, `EXT` (u8) to n zeros, `NF` (i32) to n zeros, `SE` (u8) to sa·sb zeros.
3. Coarse samples: for j from 0 below nb and i from 0 below na (j outside), p is j·na+i and sd starts at 0.0; for si from 0 below ss and sj from 0 below ss (si outside): d is col(p, (a0 plus ((i as real) plus ((si as real) plus 0.5) divided by (ss as real))·dx) plus dx·1.37e-5, (b0 plus ((j as real) plus ((sj as real) plus 0.5) divided by (ss as real))·dx) plus dx·2.91e-5); when d is below 0, `SE`[(j·ss+sj)·sa plus i·ss plus si] becomes 1; otherwise, when d is above tz, `NF`[p] grows by one and sd grows by d. Then `DEP`[p] becomes sd rounded to f32.
4. Outside flood fill, exactly as the `X` fill of rasterPlate: ne is the number of entries of `SE` that are 1; `SX` (u8) is emptied and then resized to sa·sb zeros; `SK` is emptied and then gets `reserve`(ne plus 2·sa plus 2·sb). The border list is, in order: for i from 0 below sa, i and then (sb−1)·sa plus i; then for j from 0 below sb, j·sa and then j·sa plus sa−1. Each border entry q in list order with `SE`[q] equal to 1 and `SX`[q] equal to 0 sets `SX`[q] to 1 and is pushed onto `SK`. While `SK` is not empty: q is popped from the end; i is q mod sa and j is q div sa; the neighbours in order are q−1 when i is above 0, q+1 when i is below sa−1, q−sa when j is above 0 and q+sa when j is below sb−1; each neighbour r with `SE`[r] equal to 1 and `SX`[r] equal to 0 sets `SX`[r] to 1 and is pushed.
5. Coverage: for j from 0 below nb and i from 0 below na (j outside), p is j·na+i; nx and nh start at 0; for si from 0 below ss and sj from 0 below ss (si outside), q is (j·ss+sj)·sa plus i·ss plus si; when `SX`[q] is 1, nx grows by one, and otherwise when `SE`[q] is 1, nh grows by one. m is `NF`[p] plus nh. `DEP`[p] becomes ((`DEP`[p] as a real) plus (nh as a real)·hh) divided by (m as a real), rounded to f32, when m is not 0, and 0 otherwise; `PHI`[p] becomes (m as a real) divided by (s2 as a real), rounded to f32; `EXT`[p] becomes 1 when nx equals s2 and 0 otherwise.
6. Edge refinement: `PH0` is emptied and gets a copy of `PHI` with one `append`. A pixel p (at column i and row j) is an edge when v, `PH0`[p] widened to real, is above 0 and below 1, or when any of its eight neighbours and itself (dj from −1 to 1 outside, di from −1 to 1 inside, a equal to i+di and b equal to j+dj, inside the grid) has (`PH0`[b·na+a] above 0) different from (v above 0). For j from 0 below nb and i from 0 below na (j outside), p is j·na+i; when `EXT`[p] is 0 and p is an edge: m starts at 0 and sd at 0.0; for si from 0 below 12 and sj from 0 below 12 (si outside): u is ((si as real) plus 0.5) divided by 12.0 and v is ((sj as real) plus 0.5) divided by 12.0; d is col(p, (a0 plus ((i as real) plus u)·dx) plus dx·1.37e-5, (b0 plus ((j as real) plus v)·dx) plus dx·2.91e-5); q is (j·ss plus floor(v·(ss as real)))·sa plus i·ss plus floor(u·(ss as real)) (floors as int); the sample counts when d is below 0 and `SE`[q] is 1 and `SX`[q] is 0, or when d is not below 0 and d is above tz; a counted sample grows m by one and sd by hh when d is below 0 and by d otherwise. Then `PHI`[p] becomes (m as a real) divided by 144.0 rounded to f32, and `DEP`[p] becomes sd divided by (m as a real) rounded to f32 when m is not 0, and 0 otherwise.
7. It reports `fp_view_f32(13, PHI)`, `fp_view_f32(14, DEP)` and `fp_view_u8(15, EXT)`.

The Picard field work of flow_plate.js `solvePlate` (`pres`, `flows`, `fields` and the conductance update) runs on the solve instance. More module state: `NFP` (int, flow-specified ports); `CCH`, `CL`, `ZB`, `XK`, `PRF`, `QP`, `PF`, `ZF`, `GG`, `US`, `ULL`, `UA`, `UB`, `KP0` and `TH` (f64) and `ZP`, `PXO` and `PXL` (i32) growable arrays; `PHI` and `DEP` (f32) are reused for the pixel open fraction and depth. jmin(a real, b real) real is NaN when a or b is NaN, otherwise b when b is below a, otherwise a. `tf(p int, q int) real` is ((2.0·x)·y) divided by (x plus y) with x equal to `CE`[p] and y equal to `CE`[q] when x is above 0 and y is above 0, and 0.0 otherwise. `hyp(a real, b real) real` is exactly V8's Math.hypot of two numbers: x is the absolute value of a and y the absolute value of b; nan is true when a or b is NaN; mx starts at 0.0 and becomes x when a is not NaN and x is above mx, then becomes y when b is not NaN and y is above mx; it returns infinity when mx equals infinity, NaN when nan is true, 0.0 when mx equals 0; otherwise sum and comp start at 0.0 and, for v equal to x and then y, nv is v divided by mx, su is nv·nv minus comp, pre is sum plus su, comp becomes (pre minus sum) minus su and sum becomes pre; it returns sqrt(sum)·mx.

The exported function `fp_pin(nz i32, npx i32, nfp i32)` stores `NFP`; with n equal to `NN`: `CCH`, `CL` and `ZF` are each emptied and then resized to n zeros, `ZP` to n zeros, `PHI` and `DEP` to n zeros, `ZB` to the larger of 1 and nz zeros, `PXO` to `NPT`+1 zeros, `PXL` to the larger of 1 and npx zeros, `PF` to the larger of 1 and nfp zeros and `QP` to the larger of 1 and `NPT` zeros. It reports `fp_view_f64(16, CCH)`, `fp_view_f64(17, CL)`, `fp_view_i32(18, ZP)`, `fp_view_f32(13, PHI)`, `fp_view_f32(14, DEP)`, `fp_view_f64(19, ZB)`, `fp_view_i32(20, PXO)`, `fp_view_i32(21, PXL)`, `fp_view_f64(22, PF)`, `fp_view_f64(23, QP)` and `fp_view_f64(24, ZF)`. The exported function `fp_pset()` sets every `CE`[p] (p below `NN`) to `CCH`[p] plus `CL`[p].

`pres(xs)`, with xs a read-only f64 slice, is exactly the `pres` closure: `PRF` is emptied and then resized to `NN` zeros; for each p below `NN`, `PRF`[p] becomes xs[`UID`[p]] when `UID`[p] is at least 0, otherwise `PV`[`POF`[p]] when `POF`[p] is at least 0, otherwise 0.0; it reports `fp_view_f64(30, PRF)`. `flows()` is exactly the `flows` closure with c equal to `CE` and pr equal to `PRF`: every `QP`[k] for k below `NPT` becomes 0.0; then for each port k from 0 below `NPT`, each t from `PXO`[k] below `PXO`[k+1] with p equal to `PXL`[t], i equal to p mod `NA` and j equal to p div `NA`, and each neighbour q of p in the order p−1 when i is above 0, p+1 when i is below `NA`−1, p−`NA` when j is above 0 and p+`NA` when j is below `NB`−1: when `ACT`[q] is not 0 and `POF`[q] is not k, `QP`[k] grows by tf(p, q)·(`PRF`[p] minus `PRF`[q]). The exported function `fp_flows()` runs flows().

The exported function `fp_solveq(tol real, maxit i32, k i32) i32` runs it equal to `fp_solve(tol, maxit)`; when k is 0, `XK` is emptied and then resized to (`NFP`+1)·`NU` zeros; `XK`[k·`NU` plus u] becomes `XS`[u] for every u below `NU`; then pres(`XS`) and flows() run and it returns it. The exported function `fp_comb()` sets every `XS`[u] (u below `NU`) to `XK`[u]; then for each i below `NFP` in order and each u below `NU`, `XS`[u] becomes `XS`[u] plus `PF`[i]·`XK`[(i+1)·`NU` plus u]; then pres(`XS`) runs.

The exported function `fp_fields(dxm real, lt real, kap i32)` is exactly the `fields` function of solvePlate with pr equal to `PRF` and cE equal to `CE`, except that the curvature is only computed when kap is not 0 and goes straight into `KP0` (solvePlate only ever uses the first round's curvature). `GG`, `US`, `ULL`, `UA` and `UB` are each emptied and then resized to `NN` zeros. First loop, for each p below `NN` with `ACT`[p] not 0, i equal to p mod `NA` and j equal to p div `NA`: f(q, ok) is (tf(p, q)·(`PRF`[p] minus `PRF`[q])) divided by dxm when ok is true and `ACT`[q] is not 0, and 0.0 otherwise (q is only read when ok is true); local qa is (f(p+1, i below `NA`−1) minus f(p−1, i above 0)) divided by 2.0; local qb is (f(p+`NA`, j below `NB`−1) minus f(p−`NA`, j above 0)) divided by 2.0; q is hyp(qa, qb); `GG`[p] becomes q divided by `CE`[p]; s is jmin(1.0, `CCH`[p] divided by (`CCH`[p] plus `CL`[p])) when `CCH`[p] is above 0, and 0.0 otherwise; when `CCH`[p] is above 0 and `PHI`[p] is above 0 and `DEP`[p] is above 0, kk is s divided by (((`PHI`[p] as real)·(`DEP`[p] as real))·1e-3), `UA`[p] becomes qa·kk, `UB`[p] becomes qb·kk and `US`[p] becomes q·kk; when `CL`[p] is above 0, `ULL`[p] becomes (q·(1.0 minus s)) divided by lt. The rest runs only when kap is not 0: `TH` and `KP0` are each emptied and then resized to `NN` zeros; `TH`[p] becomes fp_atan2(`UB`[p], `UA`[p]) for every p below `NN` with `US`[p] above 0. Second loop, for each p below `NN` with `US`[p] above 0, i equal to p mod `NA` and j equal to p div `NA`: th is `TH`[p]; a neighbour (q, ok) has a value when ok is true and `US`[q] is above 0, and the value is fp_atan2(fp_sin(`TH`[q] minus th), fp_cos(`TH`[q] minus th)); the x pair is (p+1, i below `NA`−1) then (p−1, i above 0), the y pair is (p+`NA`, j below `NB`−1) then (p−`NA`, j above 0); for a pair with first value v0 and second value v1, cd is (v0 minus v1) divided by (2.0·dxm) when both have values, v0 divided by dxm when only the first has one, (the negation of v1) divided by dxm when only the second has one, and 0.0 when neither has; `KP0`[p] becomes (the absolute value of (`UA`[p]·cd of the x pair plus `UB`[p]·cd of the y pair)) divided by `US`[p]. It reports `fp_view_f64(25, GG)`, `fp_view_f64(26, US)`, `fp_view_f64(27, ULL)`, `fp_view_f64(28, UA)`, `fp_view_f64(29, UB)` and `fp_view_f64(24, ZF)`.

The exported function `fp_cupd(rho real, bend i32, bk real, bl real, lt real)` is exactly the conductance update at the end of a Picard round of solvePlate, done in place on `CE`; `KP0` holds the first round's curvature. For each p below `NN`: when `ACT`[p] is 0, `CE`[p] becomes 0.0 and nothing else changes for p. Otherwise c1 starts at `CCH`[p]; when c1 is above 0: u is `US`[p] and z is `ZP`[p]; e1 is (`ZB`[z]·rho)·u when z is at least 0 and 0.0 otherwise; e2 is ((((bk·`KP0`[p]) divided by 1.5707963267948966)·rho)·u) divided by 2.0 when bend is not 0 and z is below 0, and 0.0 otherwise; c1 becomes 1.0 divided by ((1.0 divided by `CCH`[p]) plus ((e1 plus e2) divided by (((`PHI`[p] as real)·(`DEP`[p] as real))·1e-3))). c2 starts at `CL`[p]; when c2 is above 0 and bl is above 0, c2 becomes 1.0 divided by ((1.0 divided by `CL`[p]) plus (((bl·rho)·`ULL`[p]) divided by lt)). Then `CE`[p] becomes sqrt(`CE`[p]·(c1 plus c2)) and `ZF`[p] becomes c1 divided by jmax(c1 plus c2, 1e-300).
